🚽 Test case: round-20 review finding #1, the grammar-boundary half --
🚽 `type` (lang.y) is the SAME nonterminal a variable declaration's own
🚽 type comes from, so mapping `skibidi` to VAR_VOID (instead of NONE)
🚽 means `skibidi x = 5;` now parses to a VAR_VOID-typed declaration,
🚽 unless something rejects it -- ast.h's own VAR_VOID comment already
🚽 promised "no declaration syntax produces a void-typed Variable."
🚽 semantic_visit_declaration() (semantic_analyzer.c) now rejects a
🚽 VAR_VOID-typed declaration unconditionally (not just when the
🚽 initializer's type is incompatible), making that promise actually
🚽 true instead of merely assumed.
skibidi main {
    skibidi x = 5;
    bussin 0;
}
